# The Fairphone 3 as a Nerves device

## The phone

The Fairphone 3 and 3+ share one mainboard and run the same firmware.

| Part | Details |
|---|---|
| SoC | Qualcomm Snapdragon 632: 4× Cortex-A73-class + 4× Cortex-A53-class cores, aarch64 |
| Memory / storage | 4 GB RAM, 64 GB eMMC |
| Screen | 5.65" 1080×2160 |
| Cameras | Rear and front, swappable modules (FP3 and FP3+ use different sensors) |
| Loudspeaker | Swappable module: AW8898 (FP3) or TAS2557 (FP3+) |
| Radios | LTE modem with GNSS, NFC, Wi-Fi/Bluetooth |
| Sensors | Accelerometer, gyroscope, magnetometer, proximity (on the sensor DSP) |

At boot the kernel detects which camera and loudspeaker modules are
fitted, so one image works on both models:

```elixir
{out, _} = System.cmd("dmesg", [])
out |> String.split("\n") |> Enum.filter(&(&1 =~ "fp3-module-slots"))
```

## What you can use from Nerves

| Feature | Library | Notebook |
|---|---|---|
| Wi-Fi | `VintageNetWiFi` | 01 |
| Notification LED, flashlight, vibrator | `/sys/class/leds`, `rumble` | 02 |
| Sensors | `ExQcomSmgr` | 03 |
| Battery and charging | `/sys/class/power_supply` | 04 |
| Cameras (stills, H.264 stream) | `Fp3Camera` | 05 |
| NFC tags | `ExNfc` | 06 |
| GPS | `ExLocation` | 07 |
| Modem (IMEI, network, signal) | `QMI` | 08 |
| Bluetooth LE | `BlueHeron` | 09 |
| Loudspeaker, earpiece, microphone | `ExAudio`, `aplay`, `arecord` | 10, 12 |
| Screen, touchscreen, buttons | `Scenic`, `InputEvent`, `ExRatatui` + `RasterExRatatui` | 11, 13 |
| AI: Nx, object detection, speech to text, LLM chat | `nerves_ai` stack | 12 |

Not available yet: classic Bluetooth. The radio supports it, but this
firmware drives Bluetooth with BlueHeron, which only speaks Bluetooth LE,
so Bluetooth headphones, call headsets and serial adapters won't work.
Wi-Fi is set up from the Wi-Fi notebook.

## How it boots

The stock Fairphone bootloader only boots Android images, so the boot
chain has a few extra steps:

1. **Stock bootloader** loads **lk2nd** from the Android `boot` partition.
   It first applies the device-tree overlays found in the `dtbo`
   partition; Android's overlays don't match lk2nd, so `dtbo` must hold a
   *dummy* overlay.
2. **lk2nd** is a second-stage bootloader. It reads `extlinux.conf` from
   the Nerves boot partition and starts the Linux kernel.
3. An **initramfs** maps the Nerves partitions (they live inside Android's
   `userdata`, see below), mounts the read-only root filesystem and hands
   over to it.
4. **erlinit** starts the Erlang VM, and your application.

## Partitions

The Fairphone bootloader doesn't allow adding partitions, so the whole
Nerves layout is nested inside Android's `userdata` partition:

```
userdata (/dev/mmcblk0p62)
├── firmware metadata (U-Boot environment format)
├── Boot A / Boot B     ext2, 100 MiB each: kernel, device tree, initramfs
├── Rootfs A / Rootfs B squashfs, 250 MiB each: the firmware, read-only
└── Application data    f2fs, rest of the disk, mounted at /root (= /data)
```

A/B slots let `mix upload` write the inactive slot and switch to it.
Your data (notebooks, models) lives on the application partition and
survives firmware updates.

Three Android partitions are mounted read-only because they hold the
firmware the phone's DSPs and modem need: `vendor`, `persist` and `dsp`.

```elixir
File.read!("/proc/mounts") |> String.split("\n") |> Enum.filter(&(&1 =~ ~r"/(root|boot|mnt)"))
```

## First-time setup (from a stock Fairphone 3)

This wipes the phone. You need `fastboot` on your computer and these
three files:

* `lk2nd-msm8953.img` from the
  [lk2nd releases](https://github.com/msm8916-mainline/lk2nd/releases)
* the dummy `dtbo.img` from
  [z3ntu/dtbo-fp3](https://github.com/z3ntu/dtbo-fp3/releases)
* the firmware image, built with `mix firmware.image`
  (`nerves_livebook_fp3.img`)

Steps:

1. In Android, enable **Developer options → OEM unlocking**.
2. Power the phone off, connect USB, then hold **Volume Down** while
   powering on to enter fastboot mode.
3. Run:

```sh
fastboot flashing unlock        # confirm on the phone; erases it
fastboot set_active a
fastboot flash dtbo dtbo.img
fastboot flash boot lk2nd-msm8953.img
fastboot flash userdata nerves_livebook_fp3.img
fastboot reboot
```

The first boot grows the data partition to fill the phone's storage.
The AI notebooks download their models when you first run them, so
connect the phone to Wi-Fi (Wi-Fi notebook) before trying them.

## After that

* Update the firmware over the network with `mix upload`.
* Reach the phone at `http://nerves.local:4000` (Livebook) or
  `ssh nerves.local`.
* To reflash from scratch, hold **Volume Down** while powering on: once
  lk2nd is installed, that opens lk2nd's fastboot mode. Wait until the
  screen turns on before pressing the button. Then repeat the
  `fastboot flash userdata` step.
